            Debug.Assert(New_Ret == true || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1);

            
        }
    }
}
```


## Requirements

Building a contract can depend on some platform or technology specifics, here we will provide a high-level overview on how you could set up your testing automation to meet such constraints.

**.NET Foundation Code Contracts Tool:** This is a tool to statically verify your code meets given contracts. It can integrate with Visual Studio and can be used in build scripts. The official page can be accessed at: [https://github.com/Microsoft/CodeContracts](https://github.com/Microsoft/CodeContracts).

**PostSharp:** This is an AOP-based tool that you can use to automatically instrument your code. It can enhance contracts and their enforcement and offer much powerful capabilities like point-cuts and interception. The official page can be accessed at: [https://www.postsharp.net](https://www.postsharp.net).

**Visual Studio Live Unit Testing:** This tool for .NET developers can help you continuously test your code by running unit tests in the context of live edits.

**QuickWins:** This Visual Studio extension can help you to write and debug your code contracts. 

Remember, in order to generate meaningful and relevant unit test cases, you will need to understand your codebase well and be aware of potential corner cases and edge conditions.

## Conclusion
Writing contracts can be a very beneficial practice for testing, especially in large systems where there's a high chance of misbehaving code or software that behaves unexpectedly. It’s always beneficial to have a high level understanding of your software to write the appropriate contracts. After all, quality software should never be compromised under any circumstances, to this end, thorough and rigorous testing using contracts provides the most reliable way to ensure software’s stability and consistency.

