        [PexMethod(MaxRight = 200)]
        public void PUT_Contains_PexAssume([PexAssumeUnderTest("arrList != null && arrList.Count > 0")]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeUnderTest("x != 0")]int x)
        {
            bool ret = arrList.Contains(x);

            Assume(ret); // assume that the element is contained in the list

            if (ret)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
                Assert.AreEqual(x, arrList[arrList.IndexOf(x)]); // ensure that the element is still present at the same position
            }
            else
            {
                Assert.IsFalse(arrList.Contains(x));
                Assert.AreNotEqual(x, arrList.Find(y => (int)y == x)); // ensure that the element is not present in the list
            }
        }
    }
}


A: As per the documentation ArrayList does not implement the ICollection interface, so its method IndexOf does not exist. You can't directly use the ICollection property Contract.For<ICollection>(target).IndexOf(x);. 

I assume, you could just use ArrayListContract.IndexOf(target, x) as substitute. Here's the example in PexApplying:

[PexMethodAttribute]
[PexMethodUnderTest("System.Collections.ArrayList.IndexOf")]
[PexExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentException))]
public void IndexOf( 
    [PexAssumeUnderTest("arrList != null")]
    [PexArrayInput] ArrayList arrList, 
    [PexAssumeUnderTest("item != null")]
    object item) 
{
    var indexA = arrList.IndexOf(item);
    var indexB = arrList.ArrayListContract.IndexOf(arrList, item);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$Old_count_arrList", arrList.Count);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$Old_item", item);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$indexA", indexA);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$indexB",indexB);

    // Assume preconditions
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList != null);
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(item != null);
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(item));

    Assert.AreEqual(indexA, indexB);
}

This is a simple contract for the IndexOf method in ArrayList using Pex. The test uses two IndexOfs functions (class ArrayList provide arrList.ArrayListContract.IndexOf and standard ArrayList collection arrList.IndexOf) to compare if they return the same indices. Please note, if you add an item to target ArrayList, and if this item is in the list several times, both methods IndexOf will return only the first occurrence index. So, the result won't be the same for each index.
Note: 


*

*You should add at least one non-null non-primitive type object of your type for the item to your test.

*The test assumes that--------------------            Debug.Assert(New_Ret || (New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1));
            
        }
    }
}

```
This test case covers the scenario where the PUT/modification operation on 'ArrayList' is performed and at the end, a check is performed to see if the 'Contains' method (which checks for the presence of a specified value in the ArrayList) returns 'true'. The test is conducted using Pex in Visual Studio. As a result, the Pex engine automatically creates different 'Input' parameters (e.g., 'ArrayList' list and integer 'x') and verifies that the 'Contains' method returns 'true' if the 'x' is found in the 'ArrayList' list. Moreover, Pex also observes and displays the result through 'PexObserve'. Also, an assertion is introduced to provide some additional coverage to the contract condition. Thereby, the result of the ArrayList.Contains is checked to be true (i.e., if x is in arrList) or the last position of x in the old list is greater equal than -1 (i.e., x is not in arrList but we can still find some positions in the old ArrayList).

The Pex engine automatically creates a wide variety of test cases according to the specification and the conditions present in the method under test. This helps to ensure that the test case has not only covered all valid states as per the specification but also deals with boundary cases where it may encounter malformed inputs to test the robustness of the solution. Thus, Pex provides an automatic, efficient way to produce strong test cases that can verify functionality of software under many scenarios.
``` 

In summary, these testing techniques allow us to validate the correctness and robustness of our solution, and it has proven to be one of the most efficient ways to achieve this.


