A: This is probably a good example of why you should never use a single PexAssumeUnderTest attribute.
The problem is that PexAssumeUnderTest tells Pex to consider that the method under test would "assume" in an actual execution that the input parameters pass the condition specified by the attribute.
In your contract test, you are trying to prove (assert) the behavior of the ArrayList.Contains() method. This kind of proof-of-concept testing is typically done with multiple methods (each for a typical use-case), and then the results of each are combined to show that all methods work together correctly.
ArrayList.Contains() is a typical query method, and it should not alter any state – it only answers questions about the current state of data. The contract test is thus proving that the method's behavior for a specific input matches its specification (in this case, it should return true if the input is in the ArrayList and false otherwise).
So Pex can't be used on this case - its assumptions (as described by PexAssumeUnderTest) can't match the behavior of the method you presented.
However, if you really want to test this particular contract, you should divide it into individual methods and prove (assert) that they all work together correctly each with one unit test.
Please keep in mind that writing test cases is a matter of understanding and documenting the code behavior and requirements, and can be as detailed as needed for a system under test. I would not recommend overusing Pex or any other test tools because they are just helpers and don't replace the understanding of the logic needed to write a correct test.

A: I have found a way to test the Contains method, without needing Pex. 
Here the test:
[TestMethod]
    public void ArrayList_ContainsTest()
    {
        ArrayList arrayList = new ArrayList();

        arrayList.Add(5);
        Assert.IsTrue(arrayList.Contains(5));

        arrayList.Add(6);
        Assert.IsTrue(arrayList.Contains(6));

        arrayList.Add(7);
        Assert.IsTrue(arrayList.Contains(7));

        Assert.IsFalse(arrayList.Contains(8));
    }

I just added 3 numbers to the arrayList and checked if the Contains method returns true for those added elements. For the 4th test I tried to add a number that wasn't added and checked if the Contains method returns false, proving that there's no unhandled exception.
This kind of test will ensure you that the Contains method returns true if the object specified is in the ArrayList and false if it is not.
EDIT
The Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting; also needs to be added to the references for the code above to work.
(C# Visual Studio Project)

