        }
    }
}


A: As you are using Pex then your test case is quite specific, you are testing how the ArrayList.Contains method behaves, particularly with regards to it returning true or false for a given item and the original count of the ArrayList stays the same.
The conditions of your Assert.IsTrue should be met: 


*

*If ArrayList.Contains method returned false (i.e. it doesn't contain the value), then arraylist count should still be the same (i.e. no items were added or removed).

*IndexOf the value in the arraylist should still point to its position (indicating that it exists in the array).

*The LastIndexOf the value in the arraylist should still point to the same position.


If these conditions are met then Contains method behaves as expected, returns true when it should, and false otherwise.
This is a basic code of what your condition may look like:
  Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret == (New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount && New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX)

This means if the Array count hasn't change after the operation and the IndexOf and LastIndexOf values have remained consistent then contains should return true. It wouldn't necessarily mean that the ArrayList object has actually contained the value (hence the condition could also be New_arrListCount > Old_arrListCount if you also want it to return false when the element did not exist originally).
This specific Pex Contract Test can be considered correct as a meaningful verification. It confirms that the ArrayList Contains method behaves as expected under typical scenarios. You can enhance the condition by considering more possible scenarios to handle edge cases and errors. For example, Contains method should return false if the item does not exist in the list. Hence, the condition can be enhanced by: New_Ret==(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount && (New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX || New_arrListCount > Old_arrListCount) && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX).
Q: How to convert a string/text to audio on iOS? Can you provide swift example? There are many examples for converting text to speech, but what about the reverse? I'm trying to convert audio back to text.
