A: This kind of functionality isn't directly provided by iOS. However, Apple's Speech Framework can be used to convert speech to text, but not the other way.
Speech Framework in swift:
import Speech

let utterance = SpeechUtterance(string: "Hello, world.")
let speechSynthesizer = SpeechSynthesizer()
speechSynthesizer.speak(utterance) 

Unfortunately, text to speech conversion isn't directly supported in Apple's SDK or anywhere else in iOS. You'd have to create your own solution where you have to use third party services which may not provide the best quality and results that you can get from native iOS solutions.
You may need to look into APIs from services like Google Cloud Text-to-Speech.

A: Text to Audio is a complex task. Apple's speech framework can convert text to speech but conversion from audio to text has no native solution for iOS. There are third party APIs available, such as Google Cloud Text-to-Speech but the results are not always up to par. 
You may also want to look into open-source projects, such as KissFFT and KissTran to get a higher level of control over the audio processing. It will get pretty complex though. 
For iOS, Android have libraries and SDKs available for this feature.

