ms.locfileid--------------------            if (New_Ret == false)
                Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);


            // If the return value is true, we just need to make sure that last position of x in new list equals to old last position of x
            if(New_Ret == true)
                Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that If the return value is false, x wasn't added into arrList
            if (New_Ret == false)
                Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount && New_arrListIndexOfX == -1 && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);


            // If return value is true, index of x in new list should be greater or equals to old index of x
            if (New_Ret == true)
                Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}

```
  
The contract `PUT_ContainsContract` checks three properties under the assumption that `x` is being added to an ArrayList `arrList`.

Firstly, it assumes that the method `Contains` returns false when `x` is not in `arrList`. This case is checked by asserting that index `LastIndexOf(x)` in the old ArrayList is `-1`.

In case where `x` is found in the list `arrList` and the `Contains` method returns `true`, it checks that the returned value is accurate, meaning that the index `LastIndexOf(x)` in the old array list is equal to the index `LastIndexOf(x)` in the new array list.

Then, it checks that the size of the `arrList` remains the same even after addition of `x`, and checks whether the new index `IndexOf(x)` equals or greater than the old one `IndexOf(x)`.

It also checks the situation that the method `Contains` should return false when `x` was removed from `arrList`. This is checked by asserting that `Count` of the old `arrList` equals to `Count` of the new ArrayList and `IndexOf(x)` and `LastIndexOf(x)` both return `-1`.

This test-driven development (TDD) approach helps improve the code quality. The contract test will automatically suggest and suggest the edge cases where `Contains` method of ArrayList could make a failure, and thus the TDD design pattern can make our code more robust.

For another test, we can apply similar principles to other array list methods, like `Add`, `Remove`, `Insert`, `RemoveAt`, `Clear` and etc. With similar patterns, we'll be able to create extensive coverage for each of these methods.

In conclusion, this method-by-method approach using Pex will give us a fully automated test suite covering our ArrayList methods. It's a good practice to have this automated test suite for every public method your implementation provides.

For more about Pex and ArrayList, you can check out below resources:  
[Pex by Microsoft](http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/projects/pex/)  
[ArrayList Class](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.arraylist?view=netframework-4.7.2)
{% endraw %}
```
{% endhighlight %}


[n]: https://kellbot.xoxofoto.com
[np]: https://kellbot.xoxofoto.com/photoframe/
[nt]: https://kellbot.xoxofoto.com/videotech/
